[model call 2026-10-10T22:49:27Z] claude-opus-5-5 (Claude Agent SDK) self-test fix turn 29: text
I edited prompt text in `out/main-B4.workflow.json` (the identity and negotiate stages) and the JSON still loads with python3. Nothing was re-run, so none of these fixes are tested yet. I didn't change transitions, hook lines, the start code node or the account post-processing code. All five failures were real; I ignored none.

- **Sofia, run 0e329b51 (made-up product ids, a repeat promise on the same product, and closing as `no_promise` after a promise was recorded): fixed.** The prompt now says to copy `product_id` character for character from the product list and never build one from the product name. If the bank answers "unknown product_id", the agent retries once with the exact id. A product that came back ok, or with "already has a promise", counts as recorded: no second `create_promise` and no note for it. The call closes as `promise` if any `create_promise` returned ok.
- **Sofia, run c2554608 (made-up id "personal_loan_123"): fixed** by the same id-copying rule.
- **Priya, "add_note must not be called": fixed.** Brief A never uses `add_note`, but the prompt told the agent to note an "already paid" claim. Now `add_note` is only called when the account has two or more products. With one product, an "already paid" claim just closes as `paid`. The bank's data has no flag saying which brief applies, so I used the number of products to tell them apart. The catch: a Brief B customer with only one late product would now get no note.
- **Lena, job loss plus a request for lower instalments: fixed.** The hardship rule now says it comes before the restructuring rule, even when both come up in the same turn. So she is offered customer support (queue `support`, close `transferred` / `support`) rather than being transferred to restructuring.
- **Maria, swapped date then three wrong phone answers: fixed.** The agent had been reading each guess back and asking "Is that correct?" instead of checking it, so only one phone answer ever reached `verify_identity`. When she then asked for her balance, it closed as `declined`. Now every answer, including guesses like "I think" or "maybe", goes straight to `verify_identity`. Three wrong answers then lead to `not_verified` / `failed`.

